  {id:'g2-animals-1', topic:'animals', title:'The Skeleton in the Cupboard',
   cast:'Maya, Ravi, Mrs Patel and Mr Bones', cover:['egg','milk','broccoli'], badge:'Body Expert',
   img:'img/stories/g2-animals-1/', coverImg:'cover.webp',
   vocab:{
     'skeleton':'All the bones in a body, joined together.',
     'support':'To hold something up.',
     'protects':'Keeps something safe from harm.',
     'skull':'The bones of the head. The skull protects the brain.',
     'ribs':'Curved bones in the chest that make a cage around the heart and lungs.',
     'spine':'The backbone: a stack of small bones down the middle of the back.',
     'muscles':'Parts of the body that pull on bones to make them move.',
     'contracting':'Getting shorter and tighter, like a muscle pulling.',
     'exoskeleton':'A hard covering on the outside of some animals, like beetles.',
     'nutrition':'The food a living thing needs to grow and stay healthy.',
     'balanced diet':'Eating the right types of food in the right amounts.',
     'joint':'A place where two bones meet so the body can bend, like the elbow.'
   },
   pages:[
    {pic:'cover.webp', title:true, text:'The Skeleton in the Cupboard'},
    {pic:'p1.webp', text:'Everybody at school knew about the science cupboard. "There’s a skeleton in there," whispered Ravi on the first day of term. "A real one!" Maya laughed. "Let’s find out." At lunchtime, she pulled open the tall cupboard door. There, standing on a stand with wheels, was a skeleton, smiling a big, toothy smile. Ravi gulped and held on to Maya’s shoulders. Then their teacher, Mrs Patel, appeared in the doorway. "Ah, you’ve met Mr Bones," she said, smiling. "Don’t worry, he’s made of plastic. But he’s a perfect model of a human skeleton. This week, he’s going to help us find out how our bodies work."'},
    {pic:'p2.webp', text:'Mrs Patel wheeled Mr Bones into the middle of the classroom. "You have a [[skeleton]] too," she said. "It’s inside you, right now." Ravi pressed his arm. "I can feel something hard under my skin!" "That’s bone," said Mrs Patel. "Adults have 206 bones. Your skeleton has three big jobs." She counted on her fingers. "First, it supports you. Without [[support]], you’d flop like a jelly. Second, it [[protects]] your soft insides. Third, it helps you move." Maya flopped backwards over her desk like a jelly. The whole class laughed. "Exactly," smiled Mrs Patel. "That’s you without a skeleton!"'},
    {pic:'p3.webp', text:'Mrs Patel tapped Mr Bones’ head. "This is the [[skull]]. It’s like a helmet that protects your brain." She pointed to his chest. "These curved bones are your [[ribs]]. They make a cage that protects your heart and lungs." Then she pointed down his back. "This is your [[spine]], or backbone. It’s made of lots of little bones stacked up, so you can bend and twist. It also protects the nerves that carry messages from your brain to the rest of your body." Maya held Mr Bones’ hand like a friend. Ravi reached round and felt the bumps down the middle of his own back. "I can feel the little bones!" he said. "My backbone is a stack!"'},
    {pic:'p4.webp', text:'"But bones can’t move on their own," said Mrs Patel. "They need [[muscles]]." She asked Maya to bend her arm and make a muscle. Ravi put his hand on Maya’s upper arm. "It’s getting harder and fatter!" he gasped. "That’s your muscle [[contracting]]," said Mrs Patel. "Muscles are attached to bones. When a muscle contracts, it gets shorter and pulls on the bone. That’s how you move." Maya straightened her arm again. "Now a different muscle, underneath my arm, is pulling!" "Exactly," said Mrs Patel. "Muscles work in pairs. One pulls one way, and the other pulls back."'},
    {pic:'p5.webp', text:'Next, Mrs Patel showed the class the other skeletons in the science room. There was a fish skeleton with lots of thin bones, a bird skeleton with light, hollow bones for flying, and a snake skeleton that was almost all ribs and spine. "Not all animals have bones inside," said Mrs Patel. She pointed to a snail shell and a beetle under a glass dome. "These animals have a hard covering on the outside instead. It’s called an [[exoskeleton]]." Then she pointed to a jellyfish in a tall jar. "And some animals, like worms and jellyfish, have no skeleton at all!" Ravi’s eyes were huge. "So our skeleton is inside, but a beetle wears its skeleton like armour!"'},
    {pic:'p6.webp', text:'The next day, Nurse Amara came to visit. She opened a big blue lunchbox. "Your bones and muscles need the right food to grow strong," she said. "Plants can make their own food, using sunlight. But animals, including humans, can’t. We have to eat to get our [[nutrition]]." She laid the food out on the table. "Bread, rice and pasta give us energy. Fish, beans, nuts and eggs help our muscles grow and repair. Milk, yoghurt and cheese help build strong bones and teeth. Fruit and vegetables keep our whole body healthy. And a little oil gives us energy and keeps us warm too."'},
    {pic:'p7.webp', text:'"So which food is the best?" asked Maya. Nurse Amara laughed. "That’s the trick question! No single food has everything we need. We need the right types of food, in the right amounts. That’s called a [[balanced diet]]." She held up a plate divided into sections. "Lots of fruit and vegetables, some starchy food like bread, some protein like beans or fish, a little dairy like milk, and just a little oil." Maya pointed at the vegetables. "Sugary treats like cake and chocolate go on the side, just sometimes," added Nurse Amara. Ravi looked at the two biscuits in his hand. Then he picked up an apple instead. "Maybe I’ll swap a biscuit for this," he said.'},
    {pic:'p8.webp', text:'That afternoon, they went into the hall for PE. Mrs Patel had even wheeled Mr Bones in to watch. "Your skeleton and muscles work together every time you move," she said. "Let’s test it!" The children jumped high, bending their knees to land softly. They threw beanbags, feeling the muscles in their arms. Ravi tried a slow, wobbly balance on a bench. "My legs are shaking!" he said. "That’s your muscles working hard to keep you steady," said Mrs Patel. "Exercise makes muscles stronger. And the more you use your bones, the stronger they become too." Maya did a cartwheel. "My whole skeleton just went upside down!"'},
    {pic:'p9.webp', text:'On Friday, Mrs Patel set a challenge. "Make a model of a working arm." Maya and Ravi used two strips of card for the bones and a split pin for the elbow [[joint]]. Then they added two elastic bands as muscles: a red one on top and a blue one underneath. When Ravi pulled the red band, the arm bent! When he pulled the blue band, it straightened. "It works just like Maya’s arm!" he cheered. Mrs Patel clapped her hands. "You’ve shown that bones give the shape, joints let it bend, and muscles do the pulling. You’re real scientists!"'},
    {pic:'p10.webp', text:'At the end of the week, Ravi stopped Mrs Patel before she closed the cupboard. "Wait," he said. He carefully fixed a little red bow tie around Mr Bones’ neck. "For being such a good teacher." Everybody clapped and laughed, and even Mr Bones seemed to grin a bit wider. That evening, Maya ate her dinner of fish, rice, broccoli, salad and a glass of milk. "Bones, muscles, balanced diet," she said to herself. Across town, Ravi was doing star jumps in his bedroom, counting out loud. He wasn’t scared of skeletons any more. After all, he had one of his very own.'}
   ],
   understand:[
    {q:'What is Mr Bones made of?', o:['Plastic','Real bone','Wood'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'Mrs Patel says Mr Bones is made of plastic. He is a model of a human skeleton.'},
    {q:'Which bones protect the heart and lungs?', o:['The ribs','The skull','The leg bones'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'The ribs make a cage around the heart and lungs.'},
    {q:'What does contracting mean?', o:['Getting shorter and pulling','Getting longer and softer','Breaking into pieces'], a:0, type:'Word power', why:'When a muscle contracts, it gets shorter and pulls on a bone.'},
    {q:'Why do humans need to eat food?', o:['Animals cannot make their own food','Food makes our bones plastic','Only to taste nice things'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Plants make their own food using sunlight. Animals, including humans, have to eat to get nutrition.'},
    {q:'Why did Maya flop over her desk like a jelly?', o:['To show what we would be like without a skeleton','Because she was tired','Because she hurt her back'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Our skeleton supports us. Without it, we would flop like jelly.'},
    {q:'What did Maya and Ravi do straight after PE?', o:['Made a model arm','Met Nurse Amara','Looked at the fish skeleton'], a:0, type:'Order it', why:'The skeletons and food came earlier in the week. After PE, on Friday, they made the model arm.'}
   ],
   predict:{q:'Ravi pulls the red elastic band on top of the model arm. What do you think will happen?',
    o:['The arm will bend at the elbow','The arm will fall apart','Nothing will happen'], a:0,
    discover:'Pulling the top band is like a muscle contracting. It pulls on the bone, so the arm bends at the joint. Pulling the bottom band straightens it again. Real muscles work in pairs in exactly this way.'},
   sort:{title:'What does this food help with?', bins:['Energy','Growing and repairing muscles','Strong bones and teeth','Keeping the whole body healthy'],
    items:[['Bread',0,'sandwich'],['Rice',0,'rice'],['Fish',1,'trout'],['Eggs',1,'egg'],['Milk',2,'milk'],['Cheese',2,'cheese'],['Apple',3,'apple'],['Broccoli',3,'broccoli']]},
   tryit:{title:'Make a working model arm', need:['An adult to help','Two strips of thick card','A split pin','Two elastic bands','Sticky tape'],
    steps:['Ask your adult to make a small hole near the end of each card strip.','Join the strips with the split pin to make an elbow joint.','Tape one elastic band along the top of both strips, and one along the bottom.','Pull the top band. What happens? Now pull the bottom band.','Bend your own arm and feel your muscles. Compare them with your model.'],
    notice:'Which band made the arm bend? Which made it straighten? How is your model like a real arm, and how is it different?'}
  }
