  {id:'g2-rocks-1', topic:'rocks', title:'The Fossil on the Shore',
   cast:'Isla, Grandad Tom and Pebble the sheepdog', cover:['ammonite','granite','chalk'], badge:'Rock Detective',
   img:'img/stories/g2-rocks-1/', coverImg:'cover.webp',
   vocab:{
     'geologist':'A scientist who studies rocks.',
     'crystals':'Shiny, flat-sided pieces that fit together in some rocks, like granite.',
     'grains':'Tiny bits, like grains of sand or sugar.',
     'permeable':'Lets water soak into it.',
     'impermeable':'Does not let water soak in.',
     'fossil':'The remains or shape of a living thing from long ago, preserved in rock.',
     'ichthyosaur':'A huge sea reptile that lived millions of years ago, shaped a little like a dolphin.',
     'minerals':'Natural solid materials found in rocks and soil.',
     'ammonite':'A sea creature with a spiral shell that lived millions of years ago. Its fossils are found today.',
     'soil':'The top layer of the ground, made from tiny bits of rock, rotted plants and animals, air and water.',
     'organic matter':'Things that were once living, like rotting leaves, plants and dead animals.'
   },
   pages:[
    {pic:'cover.webp', title:true, text:'The Fossil on the Shore'},
    {pic:'p1.webp', text:'Every summer, Isla stayed with Grandad Tom in his little cottage by the sea. Grandad had been a [[geologist]], a scientist who studies rocks. His shelves were crammed with them: stripy ones, sparkly ones, smooth ones and crumbly ones. There were even purple crystals and curly fossils on the windowsill. "Rocks tell stories," he said, "if you know how to read them." On her first morning, Isla found him packing a bag with a magnifying glass, a notebook and a little hammer. "Where are we going?" she asked. Grandad’s moustache twitched. "Fossil hunting." Pebble the sheepdog barked and spun in a circle. He knew what that meant: the beach!'},
    {pic:'p2.webp', text:'The beach was covered in pebbles and boulders below tall, stripy cliffs. "First, let’s be rock detectives," said Grandad, kneeling down beside three rocks. "Look closely. How are they different?" Isla took out her magnifying glass. "This grey one is smooth, with tiny [[grains]], like sugar," she said. "This speckled one is sparkly, with lots of little [[crystals]] all fitted together. And this one is soft and crumbly. It has layers, like a cake." Pebble sniffed the stripy one. Grandad nodded. "Scientists group rocks by how they look, how they feel, and how they behave. You’ve just started a rock collection."'},
    {pic:'p3.webp', text:'Back at the cottage, they set up some tests on the kitchen table. "Let’s see how hard each rock is," said Grandad. Isla scratched each one with an iron nail. The sandstone scratched easily, and grains of sand fell off it. The sparkly granite didn’t scratch at all. Next, they dropped two rocks into jars of water and watched. Tiny bubbles rose from the sandstone. "It has little holes inside," said Grandad, writing in his notebook. "The water is pushing the air out. We call that [[permeable]]. Water can soak into it." The granite made no bubbles. "That one is [[impermeable]]," said Isla. "Water can’t get in!" Pebble rested his chin on the table to watch.'},
    {pic:'p4.webp', text:'Isla wrote a table in her notebook. "Granite: very hard, sparkly crystals, impermeable. Sandstone: soft, sandy grains in layers, permeable. Chalk: very soft, white and crumbly, permeable." Grandad pointed to the chalk, which was leaving white dust on the table. "Choosing the right rock matters," he said. "Granite is hard and lasts a long time, so people use it for buildings and kitchen worktops, like the one by our sink. But you wouldn’t build a wall from chalk." Isla laughed. "It would wash away in the rain!" Grandad smiled. "Exactly. Now you’re thinking like a geologist."'},
    {pic:'p5.webp', text:'That evening, they snuggled up by the fire, and Grandad opened a big book. "Over two hundred years ago, a girl named Mary Anning lived by a beach just like ours, in a town called Lyme Regis. Her family was poor, so she searched the beach for [[fossil]]s to sell. When Mary was about twelve, she and her brother found the skeleton of a huge sea creature, an [[ichthyosaur]]. It had lived millions of years ago!" Isla’s eyes went wide. "A girl found that?" "Yes," said Grandad. "But because she was a woman, many scientists didn’t give her the credit she deserved. Today, she is remembered as one of the greatest fossil hunters ever." Pebble snored softly on the rug.'},
    {pic:'p6.webp', text:'"But how does a creature turn into stone?" asked Isla. Grandad turned to some pictures in his book. "Millions of years ago, a sea creature died and sank to the seabed. Its soft body rotted away, but its hard shell was left. Slowly, layers of mud and sand covered it, like blankets." He pointed to the next picture. "Over millions of years, the layers were squashed together and turned into rock. Water seeped through, and [[minerals]] slowly took the place of the shell, turning it to stone. That’s a fossil. Then, one day, the sea wears the cliff away, and the fossil falls out onto the beach."'},
    {pic:'p7.webp', text:'The next morning, after a stormy night, they went back to the beach. "Storms wash new rocks out of the cliffs," said Grandad. "It’s the best time to look." They searched for an hour. Isla’s fingers were cold, and she was ready to give up. Then Pebble started digging and barking beside a big grey rock. Isla bent down and picked up a rock beside it. There, on its surface, was a perfect spiral, curled like a snail shell. "Grandad!" she shouted. "An [[ammonite]]!" Grandad smiled the biggest smile Isla had ever seen. The sun broke through the clouds. "This creature lived around 180 million years ago," he said quietly.'},
    {pic:'p8.webp', text:'"Can we keep it?" asked Isla. "We can," said Grandad. "Fossil hunters have a rule: only collect loose rocks on the beach, and never dig into the cliffs. Cliffs can be dangerous, and rocks can fall." Back at the cottage, Grandad knelt in his garden and dug up a trowel of [[soil]]. "Look closely," he said. Isla peered through her magnifying glass. She saw tiny bits of rock, sand and grit. She also saw a piece of an old leaf, a twig and a wriggling worm. Pebble sniffed curiously. "Soil is made from rocks!" said Isla. "Tiny bits that have broken off big rocks."'},
    {pic:'p9.webp', text:'"That’s half the answer," said Grandad. "Over thousands of years, wind, rain and ice break big rocks into smaller and smaller pieces. But soil is also made from [[organic matter]], like rotting leaves, plants and dead animals. Worms and tiny creatures help to mix it all together." He set out three jars of soil on the garden table. One was pale and sandy. One was reddish and sticky, like clay. One was dark and crumbly. "Different rocks make different soils," said Grandad. "Plants grow best in soil with lots of organic matter, because it holds water and has nutrients." Isla wrote in her notebook: Soil = tiny bits of rock + rotted plants and animals + air + water.'},
    {pic:'p10.webp', text:'On the last day of summer, Isla packed her rock collection into a wooden box, with a little space for every rock. She put her ammonite on the windowsill of her bedroom at the cottage, where the sunlight made it glow. Grandad gave her his old rock hammer. "A real geologist needs this," he said. Isla held it proudly. "When I grow up," she said, "I’m going to be a geologist, like you and like Mary Anning." Pebble wagged his tail. As Isla looked at her ammonite, she imagined an ancient sea full of curly creatures, swimming millions of years before anyone was ever born. Rocks really did tell stories.'}
   ],
   understand:[
    {q:'What does a geologist study?', o:['Rocks','Stars','Animals'], a:0, type:'Word power', why:'Grandad Tom was a geologist, a scientist who studies rocks.'},
    {q:'Which rock made bubbles in the water?', o:['Sandstone','Granite','The ammonite'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'Sandstone has tiny holes, so water pushed the air out as bubbles. It is permeable.'},
    {q:'Why is granite good for buildings?', o:['It is hard and lasts a long time','It is soft and crumbly','It soaks up water'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Granite is very hard and impermeable, so it lasts for a long time.'},
    {q:'Who was Mary Anning?', o:['A famous fossil hunter','Grandad’s sister','A sea creature'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'Mary Anning found the skeleton of an ichthyosaur and became one of the greatest fossil hunters ever.'},
    {q:'Why should fossil hunters never dig into cliffs?', o:['Rocks can fall and it is dangerous','The fossils are too small','It is too windy'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Cliffs can be dangerous, and rocks can fall. Only collect loose rocks on the beach.'},
    {q:'What did Isla and Grandad do just after finding the ammonite?', o:['Looked at soil in the garden','Tested rocks in water','Read about Mary Anning'], a:0, type:'Order it', why:'Rock tests, then Mary Anning, then the ammonite, then the soil in the garden.'}
   ],
   predict:{q:'Isla drops a piece of chalk into a jar of water. Chalk is permeable. What do you think she will see?',
    o:['Bubbles rising, as water soaks in','Nothing at all','The chalk floating'], a:0,
    discover:'Chalk is permeable: it has tiny spaces inside. Water soaks in and pushes the air out, so you see bubbles. An impermeable rock, like granite, makes no bubbles because water cannot get in.'},
   sort:{title:'Granite, sandstone or chalk?', bins:['Granite','Sandstone','Chalk'],
    items:[['Sparkly crystals',0,'granite'],['Very hard, will not scratch',0,'granite'],['Impermeable: water cannot soak in',0,'water'],['Sandy grains in layers',1,'sandstone'],['Bubbles rise from it in water',1,'sandstone'],['Soft, white and crumbly',2,'chalk'],['Leaves white dust on your fingers',2,'chalk']]},
   tryit:{title:'Be a rock detective', need:['An adult to help','Three or four different rocks or pebbles','A magnifying glass, a jar of water and an old nail or spoon','Paper and a pencil'],
    steps:['Look at each rock with a magnifying glass. Draw it. Can you see grains, crystals or layers?','Gently scratch each rock with the nail or spoon. Does it scratch easily?','Drop each rock into the jar of water. Watch closely for bubbles.','Make a table: rock, colour, grains or crystals, hard or soft, permeable or impermeable.','Group your rocks in two different ways.'],
    notice:'Which rock was the hardest? Which was permeable? Which rock would you use to build a house, and why?'}
  }
