  {id:'g2-forces-1', topic:'forces', title:'The Magnet Mystery',
   cast:'Sami, Nadia and Aunt Rosa the engineer', cover:['magnet','paperclip','key'], badge:'Force Finder',
   img:'img/stories/g2-forces-1/', coverImg:'cover.webp',
   vocab:{
     'magnet':'An object that pulls some metals towards it, like iron and steel.',
     'force':'A push or a pull.',
     'contact force':'A force that only works when two things touch, like pushing a car.',
     'non-contact force':'A force that can work without touching, like a magnet pulling a paper clip.',
     'attract':'Pull towards.',
     'magnetic':'Pulled by a magnet. Iron, steel and nickel are magnetic.',
     'poles':'The two ends of a magnet, called north and south.',
     'repel':'Push away.',
     'friction':'A force that slows things down when two surfaces rub together.',
     'fair test':'An experiment where you change only one thing and keep everything else the same.',
     'compass':'A tool with a magnetic needle that always points north.'
   },
   pages:[
    {pic:'cover.webp', title:true, text:'The Magnet Mystery'},
    {pic:'p1.webp', text:'Sami and Nadia loved visiting Aunt Rosa’s workshop. It was full of tools, wires, springs and a little metal robot. "I need your help," said Aunt Rosa. "I dropped a box of tiny screws into the sawdust on my workbench, and I can’t find them anywhere." Sami looked at the pile of sawdust. "That will take forever!" Aunt Rosa smiled and handed Nadia a big red horseshoe [[magnet]]. "Not if you use this." Nadia held the magnet over the sawdust. Click, click, click! The tiny screws jumped up out of the sawdust towards it. Sami’s mouth fell open. "It’s like magic!" "It’s not magic," said Aunt Rosa. "It’s a [[force]]."'},
    {pic:'p2.webp', text:'"A force is a push or a pull," said Aunt Rosa. She pushed Sami’s little red car along the workbench. "That’s a push. It’s a [[contact force]], because my finger touched the car." Then she put the magnet near a paper clip on the bench. The paper clip slid towards the magnet before they even touched! "That’s a pull," said Aunt Rosa. "But this time, nothing touched. Magnetic force is a [[non-contact force]]. It can work across a gap." Nadia wrote on her clipboard: Contact forces need touching. Magnetic forces can work without touching.'},
    {pic:'p3.webp', text:'"Does a magnet pull everything?" asked Sami. "Let’s test it," said Nadia. They put lots of objects on a metal tray: a paper clip, a coin, a spoon, a key, a pencil, a green button, a cork, a drinks can, a ball of foil and a nail. Nadia drew two columns on her clipboard: Attracted and Not attracted. "Predict first," she said. Sami pointed at the can and the foil. "Those are metal. The magnet will [[attract]] them for sure." One by one, Sami held the magnet over each object. The key jumped up and clicked onto the magnet! So did the paper clip and the nail. The pencil, the button and the cork didn’t move at all.'},
    {pic:'p4.webp', text:'Then Sami held the magnet over the drinks can. Nothing happened. He tried the foil. Nothing. He tried the coin. Nothing! "But they’re metal!" he said, frowning. "That’s the mystery," said Aunt Rosa. "Magnets only attract some metals. They attract iron and nickel, and steel, which is mostly iron. Anything made from those is [[magnetic]]. The can and the foil are made of aluminium, and aluminium isn’t magnetic." She held up the coin. "This coin isn’t magnetic either, but some coins have steel inside, so they do stick!" Nadia ticked the nail and the paper clip and put crosses by the can and foil. "Magnets attract some metals, but not all," she wrote.'},
    {pic:'p5.webp', text:'Aunt Rosa gave them two bar magnets on little stands. One end of each was red and marked N. The other end was blue and marked S. "Every magnet has two [[poles]]," she said. "A north pole and a south pole." Sami pushed two north poles towards each other. They pushed back! He could feel them trying to slide apart. "They don’t want to touch!" he said. "That’s called repelling," said Aunt Rosa. "Same poles [[repel]]." Then Nadia put a north pole near a south pole. Snap! They pulled together and stuck tight. "And that’s attracting," said Nadia. "Opposite poles attract!"'},
    {pic:'p6.webp', text:'"Now I’ll test you," said Aunt Rosa. She wrapped tape around the ends of one magnet so nobody could see its labels. "Which end is which?" Nadia had an idea. She held the red, north end of her own magnet near one covered end of the mystery magnet. The covered end pushed away! "That means it’s another north pole," said Nadia, "because same poles repel." Sami leaned in, grinning. Aunt Rosa clapped her hands. "Great investigation! You used what you know to make a prediction, then you tested it. Science is all about asking questions and finding clues." Sami grinned. "What will we discover next?"'},
    {pic:'p7.webp', text:'Next, they went out to the driveway. "Forces also change how things move on different surfaces," said Aunt Rosa. She set out three lanes: smooth tiles, a textured rug and rough gravel. They let Sami’s car roll down the same ramp, from the same start, onto each surface. On the smooth tiles, it zoomed a long way. On the rug, it travelled less far. On the gravel, it stopped quickly! "Rough surfaces make more [[friction]]," said Aunt Rosa. "Friction is a force that slows things down when two surfaces rub together." Nadia measured each distance with a tape measure.'},
    {pic:'p8.webp', text:'"To make it a [[fair test]], we used the same car and the same ramp, and let go from the same place each time," said Nadia. She held up her bar chart: tiles, 3 metres; rug, 1 metre; gravel, 20 centimetres. "The smoother the surface, the further the car goes." Sami thought for a moment. "So friction is why I don’t slide everywhere when I run?" "Exactly," said Aunt Rosa. "Friction between your shoes and the ground lets you grip. On a wet, slippery patch, there’s much less friction." Sami stepped on a puddle and wobbled about, pretending to skate. Everyone laughed.'},
    {pic:'p9.webp', text:'Back in the workshop, Aunt Rosa held up a [[compass]]. "The red needle always points north," she explained. "The needle is a tiny magnet, and the Earth acts like a giant magnet, so the needle lines up with it. Magnets help us find our way!" Sami turned slowly, watching the needle spin and settle. It kept pointing north! Nadia took notes. "Magnets are everywhere!" she said. Aunt Rosa pointed around the workshop: magnets holding notes on the fridge, magnets inside headphones, and even a model of a train that floats above its track using magnets. "From fridges to trains," she said, "magnets make life amazing!"'},
    {pic:'p10.webp', text:'At the end of the day, Aunt Rosa gave the twins a small bar magnet each. "For two brilliant scientists," she said. Walking home, Sami tested his magnet on the garden gate. It stuck! "And it pulls even through a gap!" he grinned. Nadia wrote it down: another discovery. That night, in his star pyjamas, Sami spotted a tiny steel screw on his bedroom floor. He held his magnet just above it. Click! The screw jumped up across the gap to meet the magnet. Sami smiled. "Magic? No… magnetism!" He wrote it in his magnet notes. Tomorrow, there would be more wonders to discover.'}
   ],
   understand:[
    {q:'How did Nadia find the tiny screws in the sawdust?', o:['With a magnet','With a brush','With a torch'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'The magnet pulled the steel screws out of the sawdust.'},
    {q:'What does repel mean?', o:['Push away','Pull towards','Stick together'], a:0, type:'Word power', why:'Two north poles repel: they push each other away.'},
    {q:'Why didn’t the drinks can stick to the magnet?', o:['It is made of aluminium, which is not magnetic','It was too heavy','It was empty'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Magnets attract iron, nickel and steel, but not aluminium.'},
    {q:'Which surface made the car stop quickest?', o:['Rough gravel','Smooth tiles','The rug'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'Gravel is rough, so there is lots of friction. The car stopped quickly.'},
    {q:'How did Nadia know the covered end was a north pole?', o:['It pushed away from a north pole','It was painted red','Aunt Rosa told her'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Same poles repel. The covered end pushed away from her north pole, so it must be north too.'},
    {q:'What did they do just after the magnet poles?', o:['The mystery magnet test','The friction challenge','Found the screws'], a:0, type:'Order it', why:'Screws, push and pull, the tray test, poles, the mystery magnet, then friction outside.'}
   ],
   predict:{q:'Sami holds a magnet over an aluminium drinks can. What do you think will happen?',
    o:['Nothing: the can will not be attracted','The can will jump onto the magnet','The can will be pushed away'], a:0,
    discover:'Aluminium is a metal, but it is not magnetic. Magnets attract only some metals: iron, nickel and steel. That is why recycling centres can use big magnets to pull steel cans out of a pile, leaving the aluminium cans behind!'},
   sort:{title:'Magnetic or not magnetic?', bins:['Magnetic (attracted)','Not magnetic'],
    items:[['Steel paper clip',0,'paperclip'],['Iron nail',0,'nail'],['Steel key',0,'key'],['Aluminium can',1,'can'],['Cork',1,'cork'],['Plastic button',1,'button'],['Pencil',1,'pencil'],['Glass marble',1,'marble']]},
   tryit:{title:'The friction ramp test', need:['An adult to help','A toy car','A tray or a large book to make a ramp','Three surfaces: a smooth floor, a towel or rug, and a doormat','A tape measure'],
    steps:['Rest the ramp on a pile of books so it slopes.','Let the car roll down onto the smooth floor. Do not push it. Measure how far it goes.','Lay the towel at the bottom of the ramp and try again. Measure.','Try the doormat. Keep the car, ramp and starting place the same each time.','Draw a bar chart of your results.'],
    notice:'Which surface let the car travel furthest? Which had the most friction? Why do you think shoes have bumpy soles?'}
  }
  ,
  {id:'g2-plants-2', topic:'plants', title:'The Seed That Travelled',
   cast:'Layla, Mr Okoro the ranger and Pip the seed', cover:['dandelion','sycamore','blackberry'], badge:'Seed Explorer',
   img:'img/stories/g2-plants-2/', coverImg:'cover.webp',
   vocab:{
     'ranger':'A person whose job is to look after a park or countryside.',
     'pollen':'A yellow dust made by flowers. Moving pollen between flowers helps them make seeds.',
     'pollination':'When pollen is carried from one flower to another flower of the same kind, so seeds can form.',
     'nectar':'A sweet liquid inside flowers that bees and other insects drink.',
     'seed case':'The part around seeds that protects them, like a pod or a fruit.',
     'seed dispersal':'The different ways seeds travel away from the parent plant.',
     'burrs':'Prickly seeds with tiny hooks that stick to fur and clothes.',
     'life cycle':'The stages a living thing goes through, again and again.',
     'germinates':'Starts to grow from a seed.'
   },
   pages:[
    {pic:'cover.webp', title:true, text:'The Seed That Travelled'},
    {pic:'p1.webp', text:'Layla’s class went on a trip to Greenvale Country Park. Mr Okoro, the park [[ranger]], met them in a meadow full of wildflowers and butterflies. "Today’s mission," he said, "is to find out how plants make new plants, and how their seeds travel." Layla’s hand shot up. "But plants can’t walk! How can their seeds travel?" Mr Okoro smiled. "That’s exactly what we’re going to find out." Layla blew gently on a fluffy dandelion clock. Hundreds of tiny seeds floated away on little parachutes. One landed on her arm. She looked at it through her magnifying glass. "Hello," she whispered. "I’ll call you Pip."'},
    {pic:'p2.webp', text:'"Before Pip was a seed," said Mr Okoro, "it was part of a flower." He knelt beside a bright yellow flower and pointed inside. "Look closely. Around the middle are thin stalks with yellow dust on top. That dust is called [[pollen]]. And right in the centre is the part that will grow the seeds." Layla peered through her magnifying glass. "So the flower is like a seed factory?" "Exactly," said Mr Okoro. "But to make seeds, most flowers need pollen from another flower of the same kind. That’s called [[pollination]]."'},
    {pic:'p3.webp', text:'"How does pollen get from one flower to another?" asked Layla. Just then, a big, fuzzy bumblebee buzzed down to a pink flower. It wriggled inside to drink the sweet [[nectar]]. When it flew off, its legs and body were dusted with yellow pollen. "The bee is carrying pollen!" said Layla, pointing. "That’s right," said Mr Okoro. "Flowers have bright petals and sweet smells to attract insects. When the bee visits the next flower, some of the pollen rubs off. The insects get food, and the flowers get pollinated." Layla grinned. "It’s a deal!"'},
    {pic:'p4.webp', text:'Then Mr Okoro led them into the tall grasses. He shook a grass stem gently, and a cloud of yellow dust puffed into the air. "Achoo!" sneezed Layla. Her friends burst out laughing. "Not all flowers need insects," said Mr Okoro. "Grass flowers are small and plain, without bright petals. They let the wind carry their pollen instead." Layla wiped her nose with a tissue. "That’s why some people sneeze in summer!" "Yes," laughed Mr Okoro. "Lots of pollen floats in the air." Layla wrote in her notebook: Pollen can be carried by insects or by the wind.'},
    {pic:'p5.webp', text:'"Once a flower has been pollinated," said Mr Okoro, "its petals dry up and fall off. Then seeds start to grow inside." He picked up a dry poppy head. When he shook it, tiny black seeds sprinkled out of little holes at the top, like a pepper pot. Layla caught them in her hands. On a log, he had laid out a cut apple, a tomato and a pea pod. "Seeds are often inside a fruit or a [[seed case]], which protects them," he said. Layla looked closely. "The apple has seeds, the tomato has seeds and the pea pod has seeds!" "Exactly," said Mr Okoro. "Fruit isn’t just for eating. It’s how plants protect their seeds."'},
    {pic:'p6.webp', text:'"But why do seeds need to travel?" asked Layla, holding up Pip. Mr Okoro pointed at a huge oak tree. In its deep shade, lots of little oak seedlings were crowded together. "If all its acorns fall right underneath it, the young trees are stuck in the shade. They have to fight over light, water and space. So plants have clever ways to send their seeds far away. We call that [[seed dispersal]]." Layla looked at Pip. "So you’re travelling to find your own space to grow?" She imagined Pip giving a tiny nod. "Pip uses the wind," she said. "What are the other ways?"'},
    {pic:'p7.webp', text:'In the wood, they found sycamore trees. Their seeds had wings, like little helicopters. Layla threw one high into the air, and it spun round and round as it floated down. "Wind dispersal!" she said. As they walked on, her socks got covered in prickly little [[burrs]]. "Ouch! They’re sticking to me!" "Those are seeds too," laughed Mr Okoro. "They have tiny hooks that grab onto animals’ fur. The animal carries them far away, and later they fall off." Layla picked off the burrs one by one. "So I’m helping plants travel too!"'},
    {pic:'p8.webp', text:'Next, they found a bramble bush covered in juicy blackberries. A blackbird was gobbling them up. "Birds eat the fruit," said Mr Okoro, "but the seeds pass through their bodies. Later, the bird drops them somewhere new, ready to grow." Down by the stream, a fluffy seed landed on the water and was carried along. "Some seeds travel by water," said Mr Okoro. "Coconuts can even float across oceans!" Then he showed them a dry pod on the bank. Pop! It burst open and flicked its seeds through the air. "And some plants burst their seed cases open to shoot their seeds out." Layla’s mouth made a perfect O.'},
    {pic:'p9.webp', text:'At the end of the day, the class sat in a circle on the grass. Layla held up her notebook. She had drawn a big circle with arrows. "Here’s the [[life cycle]] of a flowering plant," she said. "A seed [[germinates]] and grows into a plant. The plant grows flowers. The flowers are pollinated by insects or by the wind. Then seeds form inside a fruit or seed case. Then the seeds are dispersed: by wind, by animals, by water, or by bursting. And then the seeds grow into new plants!" Everybody clapped. Mr Okoro tipped his hat. "That’s a perfect cycle, Layla."'},
    {pic:'p10.webp', text:'On the coach home, Layla sat by the window with her notebook on her lap. Pip was still on her sleeve. She opened the window just a crack. "Good luck, Pip," she whispered. "Find a sunny spot with lots of space." A gentle breeze lifted Pip up and away, over the hedges and fields. Layla rested her chin on her hand and watched until Pip was just a tiny white speck. Mr Okoro smiled from the seat behind. The next spring, a bright yellow dandelion bloomed in a sunny field far from Greenvale Park. Nobody knew where it had come from. But Layla would have smiled. Pip had found the perfect place to grow.'}
   ],
   understand:[
    {q:'What is Pip?', o:['A dandelion seed','A bumblebee','A blackbird'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'Pip is a tiny dandelion seed with a fluffy parachute that landed on Layla’s arm.'},
    {q:'What does pollination mean?', o:['Pollen being carried to another flower of the same kind','A seed starting to grow','Fruit falling off a tree'], a:0, type:'Word power', why:'Pollination is when pollen moves from flower to flower, so seeds can form.'},
    {q:'Why did Layla sneeze in the long grass?', o:['Grass pollen was blowing in the air','She had a cold','A bee tickled her nose'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Grass flowers use the wind to carry pollen, so lots of it floats in the air.'},
    {q:'How do burrs travel?', o:['They hook onto animals’ fur','They float on water','They spin like helicopters'], a:0, type:'Find it', why:'Burrs have tiny hooks that grab onto fur, or socks!'},
    {q:'Why do seeds need to travel away from the parent plant?', o:['To find their own light, water and space','Because they are scared of trees','To find the sea'], a:0, type:'Think about it', why:'Seeds that land right under the parent are crowded and shaded, so they struggle to grow.'},
    {q:'What happens just after a flower is pollinated?', o:['Seeds start to form','The plant germinates','The seeds are dispersed'], a:0, type:'Order it', why:'Seed, plant, flower, pollination, seeds form, dispersal, and round again.'}
   ],
   predict:{q:'Layla drops a sycamore seed from a high bridge on a windy day. Where do you think it will land?',
    o:['A long way from the bridge','Straight down, right underneath','It will float back up into the tree'], a:0,
    discover:'A sycamore seed has wings, so it spins slowly as it falls. The slower it falls, the longer the wind has to carry it, so it lands further away. That is wind dispersal: the seed gets its own space, away from the parent tree.'},
   sort:{title:'How does each seed travel?', bins:['Wind','Animals','Water','Bursting open'],
    items:[['Dandelion seed',0,'dandelion'],['Sycamore seed',0,'sycamore'],['Prickly burr',1,'burr'],['Blackberry',1,'blackberry'],['Coconut',2,'coconut'],['Pea pod',3,'beanpod']]},
   tryit:{title:'Design a seed that flies', need:['An adult to help','Paper, scissors and paper clips','A stopwatch or timer'],
    steps:['Cut a strip of paper about 15 cm long and 4 cm wide.','Cut halfway down the middle, then fold the two flaps opposite ways, like wings.','Add a paper clip to the bottom to be the seed.','Drop it from the same height each time and time how long it takes to land.','Try longer wings, shorter wings, or two paper clips. Which falls slowest?'],
    notice:'Which design stayed in the air longest? Why would a slow-falling seed travel further on a windy day?'}
  }
